kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinux ("AcceleratedVideoDecoder") defaults on
only when USE_VAAPI is set. A V4L2-only desktop build compiles the
decoder and then leaves the feature off, so playback stays on the
software decoder unless every caller passes --enable-features. The
Omarchy kiosk enables AcceleratedVideoEncoder and does not pass
AcceleratedVideoDecoder, because the VA-API default already covers it.

Default the feature on when this binary includes VA-API or V4L2.
x86_64 stays on (use_vaapi is true there). A build with neither stays
off. AcceleratedVideoEncoder stays off by default; the kiosk already
passes that feature, and CreateV4L2VEA() checks it.

media/base/media_switches.h includes media/gpu/buildflags.h, so
USE_V4L2_CODEC is visible here.

--- a/media/base/media_switches.cc
+++ b/media/base/media_switches.cc
@@ -739,10 +739,12 @@
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
 // Enable vaapi/v4l2 video decoding on linux. This is already enabled by default
 // on chromeos, but needs an experiment on linux.
+// On when this build includes VA-API or V4L2. A V4L2-only desktop build
+// otherwise leaves hardware decode disabled even though the decoder is linked.
 BASE_FEATURE(kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinux,
              "AcceleratedVideoDecoder",
-#if BUILDFLAG(USE_VAAPI)
+#if BUILDFLAG(USE_VAAPI) || BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC)
              base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 #else
              base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 #endif
